<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>The matrix <m>M = {\left[\begin{array}{cc} -1 &amp; -1 \\ 3 &amp; 1 \end{array}\right]}</m> maps a point P onto <m>P'({-3},\,{5})</m>.</p><p>(a) Find <m>M^{-1}</m>.</p><div><fillin characters="3" name="ans1"/>  </div><p>(b) Find the coordinates of P.</p><div>( <fillin characters="5" name="ans2"/> , <fillin characters="5" name="ans3"/> )   </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) <m>\det M = ad - bc = {2}</m>. Swap a and d, change the signs of b and c, and divide by the determinant:</p><p><m>M^{-1} = \dfrac{1}{{2}}{\left[\begin{array}{cc} 1 &amp; 1 \\ -3 &amp; -1 \end{array}\right]} = {\left[\begin{array}{cc} \frac{1}{2} &amp; \frac{1}{2} \\ -\frac{3}{2} &amp; -\frac{1}{2} \end{array}\right]}</m></p><p>(b) The inverse undoes the transformation, so it sends the image point back to where it came from: <m>M^{-1}{\left[\begin{array}{c} -3 \\ 5 \end{array}\right]} = {\left[\begin{array}{c} 1 \\ 2 \end{array}\right]}</m>. So P is <m>({1},\,{2})</m>.</p><p>Check: <m>{\left[\begin{array}{cc} -1 &amp; -1 \\ 3 &amp; 1 \end{array}\right]}{\left[\begin{array}{c} 1 \\ 2 \end{array}\right]} = {\left[\begin{array}{c} -3 \\ 5 \end{array}\right]}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>\left[\begin{array}{cc} \frac{1}{2} &amp; \frac{1}{2} \\ -\frac{3}{2} &amp; -\frac{1}{2} \end{array}\right]</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>1</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>2</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>